1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:03,980
Friday, the twenty-first of January, 1994.

2
00:00:04,280 --> 00:00:08,655
A man cuts his beach holiday
short and heads back to Santiago.

3
00:00:08,947 --> 00:00:12,122
He asks to see his boss.
He says it's urgent.

4
00:00:12,413 --> 00:00:14,119
That afternoon, he explains.

5
00:00:14,119 --> 00:00:17,834
A few months ago, he says, he
made a mistake on his computer.

6
00:00:17,834 --> 00:00:21,063
He entered a sale as a
purchase. Buy instead of sell.

7
00:00:21,380 --> 00:00:24,105
The mistake cost thirty million dollars.

8
00:00:24,413 --> 00:00:27,613
And instead of reporting
it, he tried to win it back.

9
00:00:32,913 --> 00:00:36,648
The money belonged to Codelco,
Chile's state copper company.

10
00:00:36,648 --> 00:00:39,138
The biggest copper company in the world.

11
00:00:39,447 --> 00:00:42,717
Codelco mined about a tenth
of all the world's copper.

12
00:00:42,717 --> 00:00:46,472
Its sales brought in about a
fifth of Chile's export earnings.

13
00:00:46,780 --> 00:00:50,348
Chile had nationalised
its copper mines in 1971.

14
00:00:50,348 --> 00:00:55,030
President Salvador Allende called
copper "the salary of Chile".

15
00:00:55,347 --> 00:00:59,347
Codelco was not just a company.
It was the country's wallet.

16
00:00:59,647 --> 00:01:04,532
The man who lost the money was its head
of futures trading. Juan Pablo Dávila.

17
00:01:04,532 --> 00:01:05,722
He was thirty-four.

18
00:01:06,013 --> 00:01:10,338
He was an accountant. He had joined
Codelco in 1986 as a trainee,

19
00:01:10,338 --> 00:01:14,863
on a salary of eight thousand six
hundred and eighty dollars a year.

20
00:01:15,180 --> 00:01:19,080
Colleagues sometimes called
him J.P. As in J.P. Morgan.

21
00:01:20,380 --> 00:01:25,775
Codelco had used the futures market
since 1983, for one main job. Protection.

22
00:01:25,775 --> 00:01:31,030
A miner sells its future copper at
today's price, so a crash can't hurt it.

23
00:01:31,347 --> 00:01:34,194
In 1990, the company
allowed something more.

24
00:01:34,194 --> 00:01:37,494
A small amount of real
trading, just to make money.

25
00:01:37,494 --> 00:01:41,247
No more than ten thousand
tonnes. And a strict loss limit.

26
00:01:41,547 --> 00:01:44,302
In August 1992, a committee set the rules.

27
00:01:44,302 --> 00:01:47,976
An open position of no more
than twenty thousand tonnes.

28
00:01:47,976 --> 00:01:51,255
No more than a million
dollars at risk on any day.

29
00:01:51,255 --> 00:01:56,372
And an automatic stop-loss order on every
trade, to close it if it went wrong.

30
00:01:56,680 --> 00:02:01,180
According to the state auditors, it was
the committee's last recorded meeting.

31
00:02:02,480 --> 00:02:06,568
Here is how it worked in practice.
Dávila made the trades by phone.

32
00:02:06,568 --> 00:02:11,205
The brokers confirmed them by fax. And
Dávila typed them into a spreadsheet.

33
00:02:11,513 --> 00:02:15,763
That spreadsheet was the company's
record. The accounts were built from it.

34
00:02:16,080 --> 00:02:20,451
He kept it on his office computer,
and on his own personal computer.

35
00:02:20,451 --> 00:02:23,280
He used his own fax,
and his own phone line.

36
00:02:23,580 --> 00:02:28,594
In 1991, Codelco had bought a proper
system to control the trading.

37
00:02:28,594 --> 00:02:30,764
By April 1993 it was working.

38
00:02:30,764 --> 00:02:35,105
It was never used, because
nobody typed in the old trades.

39
00:02:35,413 --> 00:02:40,488
So the only person who knew what the trading
desk was doing was the man running it.

40
00:02:41,780 --> 00:02:46,405
By September 1993, copper had been
falling for more than a year.

41
00:02:46,713 --> 00:02:50,913
That month, by Dávila's own account,
he recorded a sale as a purchase.

42
00:02:50,913 --> 00:02:54,513
When he found the error, it had
cost thirty million dollars.

43
00:02:54,813 --> 00:02:58,400
Thirty times the most he was
allowed to risk in a single day.

44
00:02:58,400 --> 00:03:01,163
One phone call to his
boss would have ended it.

45
00:03:01,447 --> 00:03:03,197
He didn't make the call.

46
00:03:03,480 --> 00:03:06,612
Instead, he decided to
trade his way out. Quietly.

47
00:03:06,612 --> 00:03:09,055
Betting that copper would keep falling.

48
00:03:10,347 --> 00:03:13,962
His bets were short positions.
Sell now, buy back later.

49
00:03:13,962 --> 00:03:17,447
If the price falls, you
profit. If it rises, you lose.

50
00:03:17,747 --> 00:03:21,596
Over the next four months he made
more than five thousand trades,

51
00:03:21,596 --> 00:03:24,497
with twenty-three brokers
in London and New York.

52
00:03:24,813 --> 00:03:27,222
Copper, and then gold and silver too.

53
00:03:27,222 --> 00:03:30,738
When one broker's credit ran
out, he moved to another.

54
00:03:31,047 --> 00:03:35,824
Some of it, investigators later said,
he ran from his beach house in Reñaca.

55
00:03:35,824 --> 00:03:39,847
He had the brokers send the
paperwork to him, not to the office.

56
00:03:40,147 --> 00:03:43,289
In November, copper hit
its lowest price in years.

57
00:03:43,289 --> 00:03:47,247
Sixteen hundred and thirty dollars
a tonne. And then it turned.

58
00:03:48,547 --> 00:03:49,833
That same November,

59
00:03:49,833 --> 00:03:55,316
two of his bosses heard that the desk was
carrying positions for 1994 with losses

60
00:03:55,316 --> 00:03:57,347
of around ten million dollars.

61
00:03:57,647 --> 00:04:00,640
According to the auditors,
they told him, verbally,

62
00:04:00,640 --> 00:04:03,047
to book the loss in that year's accounts.

63
00:04:03,347 --> 00:04:05,522
He didn't. And nobody checked.

64
00:04:05,813 --> 00:04:09,707
On the other side of the trades,
one broker was getting nervous.

65
00:04:09,707 --> 00:04:13,844
Wolfgang Becker, of Merrill Lynch,
was one of Dávila's main brokers.

66
00:04:13,844 --> 00:04:15,913
Dávila was asking for more credit.

67
00:04:16,213 --> 00:04:18,723
Becker later told the Wall Street Journal,

68
00:04:18,723 --> 00:04:21,413
I had the impression
something was not right.

69
00:04:22,713 --> 00:04:25,138
Which brings us back to that Friday.

70
00:04:25,447 --> 00:04:29,852
Dávila told the sales manager, Owen
Guerrini, everything. The wrong key.

71
00:04:29,852 --> 00:04:33,522
The thirty million. The cover-up.
The trades to win it back.

72
00:04:33,813 --> 00:04:37,638
He thought the loss was now
around a hundred million dollars.

73
00:04:37,947 --> 00:04:42,440
Guerrini went straight up the chain.
Emergency meetings ran all weekend.

74
00:04:42,440 --> 00:04:45,872
Codelco's heads in London
and New York were flown home.

75
00:04:46,180 --> 00:04:49,277
On Monday, the company
filed a criminal complaint.

76
00:04:49,277 --> 00:04:52,127
Auditors from Price
Waterhouse were called in.

77
00:04:52,127 --> 00:04:56,030
And Merrill Lynch's Becker helped
Codelco unwind the positions.

78
00:04:56,347 --> 00:05:00,757
It was worse than a hundred million.
When Codelco closed the positions,

79
00:05:00,757 --> 00:05:04,422
the bill came to about two
hundred and six million dollars.

80
00:05:04,713 --> 00:05:09,588
Forty-four percent of the
company's entire profit for 1993.

81
00:05:10,880 --> 00:05:14,559
On the twenty-sixth of January,
Codelco told the country.

82
00:05:14,559 --> 00:05:16,947
The executives above Dávila resigned.

83
00:05:16,947 --> 00:05:19,980
Ten days later, so did
the head of the company.

84
00:05:20,280 --> 00:05:25,036
The press called it Coppergate. The opposition
threatened to impeach two ministers.

85
00:05:25,036 --> 00:05:28,130
Business groups said it proved
Codelco should be sold.

86
00:05:28,447 --> 00:05:32,996
One Codelco director said it was hard
to believe one person could do all this

87
00:05:32,996 --> 00:05:36,422
without anyone else knowing.
He would have to be a genius.

88
00:05:36,713 --> 00:05:40,007
Dávila said, I don't believe
I've committed any crime.

89
00:05:40,007 --> 00:05:43,788
I made a mistake, and I am going
to assume the responsibility.

90
00:05:44,080 --> 00:05:46,123
And Chile got a new word. Davilar.

91
00:05:46,123 --> 00:05:50,390
To botch things up miserably, as the
Wall Street Journal translated it.

92
00:05:50,390 --> 00:05:53,696
When you get something
catastrophically wrong in Chile,

93
00:05:53,696 --> 00:05:56,280
you don't just make a
mistake. You davilar.

94
00:05:57,580 --> 00:06:01,305
But investigators did not
believe it was just a mistake.

95
00:06:01,613 --> 00:06:05,935
Codelco went through his bank
accounts. It said that from 1990,

96
00:06:05,935 --> 00:06:08,404
large deposits had appeared in them,

97
00:06:08,404 --> 00:06:12,863
at least three point two million
dollars by 1993, mostly in cash.

98
00:06:13,180 --> 00:06:15,311
Some of the money, Codelco said,

99
00:06:15,311 --> 00:06:19,973
came from accounts linked to the
representatives of one London broker,

100
00:06:19,973 --> 00:06:20,972
Sogemin Metals.

101
00:06:20,972 --> 00:06:26,166
In 1990, its business with Codelco had
been under two hundred thousand tonnes.

102
00:06:26,166 --> 00:06:28,830
In 1993, it was two point three million.

103
00:06:29,147 --> 00:06:31,116
Codelco also said that in 1990,

104
00:06:31,116 --> 00:06:36,135
one and a half million dollars had moved
from a subsidiary of the German metals

105
00:06:36,135 --> 00:06:41,472
giant Metallgesellschaft into an account in
the Cayman Islands controlled by Dávila.

106
00:06:41,780 --> 00:06:42,580
In lawsuits,

107
00:06:42,530 --> 00:06:47,280
Codelco alleged he had made losing trades
for Codelco and winning trades for

108
00:06:47,280 --> 00:06:49,155
brokers, and shared the money.

109
00:06:49,447 --> 00:06:50,482
Dávila denied it.

110
00:06:50,482 --> 00:06:54,622
His lawyer said the deposits were
loans, and called him a scapegoat.

111
00:06:54,913 --> 00:06:59,045
The judge who investigated the case,
José Benquis, put it more bluntly.

112
00:06:59,045 --> 00:07:01,665
The story of the slip
of the finger, he said,

113
00:07:01,665 --> 00:07:06,088
was so hard to swallow that he suspected
at once there was something hidden.

114
00:07:07,380 --> 00:07:09,408
In March 1994 he was charged.

115
00:07:09,408 --> 00:07:13,255
He would spend the next twelve
years in Chile's courts.

116
00:07:13,547 --> 00:07:17,639
First he served a separate
sentence for false tax returns.

117
00:07:17,639 --> 00:07:23,566
In 1999, a judge sentenced him to eight
years for the losses. On appeal, it was cut.

118
00:07:23,566 --> 00:07:26,247
In 2006, the Supreme Court settled it.

119
00:07:26,547 --> 00:07:28,630
Guilty of fraud against the state.

120
00:07:28,630 --> 00:07:32,979
And guilty of dealing with brokers
where he had a conflict of interest.

121
00:07:32,979 --> 00:07:36,042
Three years, plus two terms
of eight hundred days.

122
00:07:36,042 --> 00:07:40,147
And a hundred and eighty-six million
dollars in damages to Codelco.

123
00:07:40,447 --> 00:07:45,872
In January 2007, he handed himself in
at a prison in the south of Chile.

124
00:07:46,180 --> 00:07:51,424
By then, Codelco had sued and settled with
brokers in London and the United States,

125
00:07:51,424 --> 00:07:54,330
and recovered about a
hundred million dollars.

126
00:07:55,647 --> 00:07:57,282
And here is the last twist.

127
00:07:57,282 --> 00:08:02,066
Codelco later said that if copper had
fallen, his trades would have made money.

128
00:08:02,066 --> 00:08:06,245
It didn't. Through 1994 it climbed
to three thousand dollars a tonne.

129
00:08:06,245 --> 00:08:09,697
If he had held on, the losses
would have been far bigger.

130
00:08:10,013 --> 00:08:13,261
Was it a slip of the little
finger, followed by panic?

131
00:08:13,261 --> 00:08:18,013
Or a cover story for something else? A
court decided. Chile never quite agreed.

132
00:08:18,313 --> 00:08:21,850
But the auditors found something
that didn't depend on the answer.

133
00:08:21,850 --> 00:08:25,494
One man made the trades, recorded
the trades and checked the trades.

134
00:08:25,494 --> 00:08:27,316
The committee had stopped meeting.

135
00:08:27,316 --> 00:08:30,263
The system that could have
caught him sat switched off.

136
00:08:30,580 --> 00:08:35,355
The wrong key cost thirty million dollars.
Everything after that was a choice.

